{"episodes":{"200905":"A multi-day rain event caused by a slow-moving upper-level low resulted in 1-2.5 inches of rain across central Pennsylvania. Clearfield County saw the most rain with this system, with Coal Run near DuBois briefly leaving it's banks and temporarily closing one road.","200947":"A late season nor'easter brough gusty winds with sporadic damage to southern New England along with rainfall amounts of widespread 2-3 inches and a swath of 5-7 inches over southeast MA.","201001":"A very hot atmosphere on the afternoon of the 22nd combined with a variety of surface boundaries creating isolated thunderstorms, mostly east of the caprock escarpment. Although cloud bases were very high, ample instability in mid-levels of the atmosphere allowed storms to quickly become severe with large hail. One storm in southeastern Crosby County produced hail up to half dollar size. Several other thunderstorms likely produced severe hail but no reports were received.","201328":"An isolated strong thunderstorm produced gale-force wind gusts and large hail in the vicinity of southern Maryland.","201335":"An isolated severe thunderstorm produced large hail over southern Maryland.","201958":"A developing surface low and dryline across the Permian Basin produced scattered strong to severe thunderstorms.","202170":"An upper-level shortwave trough generated thunderstorms on the warm side of a stationary front that was stretched across Central Texas. Some of these storms moved into South-Central Texas and produced large hail and a few damaging wind gusts.","202435":"Multiple rounds of hail-producing thunderstorms impacted portions of the WFO Norman Forecast Area on the 22nd. Morning convection was initiated by moist isentropic ascent in low to mid-levels. With sufficient elevated instability and wind shear already in place, a few transiently organized thunderstorms produced hail from south-central into north-central Oklahoma. Thunderstorm intensity gradually increased towards midday, culminating in the development of a more robust, right-moving supercell thunderstorm over Carter and Love Counties. This storm generated multiple reports of very large hail, thunderstorm wind damage and flooding. ||Following a brief early afternoon lull, additional thunderstorms developed by late afternoon across southwestern Oklahoma and western-north Texas. This activity was initiated near convective outflow boundaries from prior thunderstorms and enhanced by strong surface heating. The resulting environment featured strong instability, steep lapse rates, and favorable deep-layer wind shear for organized thunderstorms. Multiple splitting supercell thunderstorms emerged in this regime, producing widespread reports of large to very large hail during the evening.","202436":"Thunderstorms developed by late afternoon of the 22nd across southwestern Oklahoma and western-north Texas. This activity was initiated near convective outflow boundaries from prior thunderstorms and enhanced by strong surface heating. The resulting environment featured strong instability, steep lapse rates, and favorable deep-layer wind shear for organized thunderstorms. Multiple splitting supercell thunderstorms emerged in this regime, producing widespread reports of large to very large hail during the evening.","202602":"A cold front that moved into the region on May 21st had stalled over the Louisiana coast by the 22nd. Strong daytime heating enhanced convective updrafts while temperatures aloft remain quite cool for the time of year. This environment was sufficient to support a severe thunderstorm with large hail.","202636":"Scattered thunderstorms developed over southwest Missouri during the evening of May 21, 2025. Although most storms remained sub-severe, one thunderstorm reached severe limits, producing quarter size hail in Jasper County.","202711":"As surface moisture increased, instability surged by sunset. Convective initiation was a result of moisture advection ahead of a frontal boundary. Storms had access to adequate bulk shear and remaining instability to provide a threat for sporadic damaging winds and hail.","202812":"A secondary cold front and upper level trough brought scattered severe thunderstorms to southeast Virginia in the late afternoon and evening. These storms mainly produced large hail, with a few reports of golf ball size hail and larger.","203064":"On May 22, strong to severe thunderstorms developed across western North Texas, driven by a northward-moving warm front and a southward-propagating upper-level disturbance. Areas extending from Gainesville southwest to Eastland experienced occasional severe weather impacts, including large hail and damaging wind gusts.","203089":"An occluding surface low slowly drifted across Ohio on May 21st. Bands of showers with embedded thunderstorms moved across southwest Pennsylvania. During the mid to late afternoon, one convective band, associated with ample 0-3km CAPE and surface vorticity, was able to generate four tornadoes across southwest PA.  Three were relatively brief and weak, but one longer-track EF1 was noted in Washington County. Multiple rounds of slow-moving heavier rain also produced a number of flash flooding incidents.","203388":"Scattered thunderstorms developed in far southeast Kansas in the overnight hours from the 21st into the 22nd. A couple of the storms strengthened with quarter sized hail being reported in Parsons by a trained spotter."},"events":[{"begin":"2025-05-22T00:59Z","county":"Butler","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2025-05-22T03:15Z","endLat":40.8451,"endLon":-79.9895,"episode":203089,"id":1266331,"injuries":0,"lat":40.8472,"location":"0.98 WSW Highfield","lon":-79.9885,"narrative":"Road was flooded just west of the Butler Farm Market.","source":"911 Call Center","state":"PA","type":"Flash Flood","wfo":"PBZ"},{"begin":"2025-05-22T01:00Z","county":"Lawrence","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"